{"name":"Lake Thun","hex":"#44bbdd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Lake Thun","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A lake in the Bernese Oberland, Switzerland, which takes its name from the town of Thun."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Lake_Thun"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lake Thun","blocks":[{"text":"Lake Thun is an Alpine lake in the Bernese Oberland in Switzerland named after the city of Thun, on its northern shore. At 48.3 km2 (18.6 sq mi) in surface area, it is the largest Swiss lake entirely within a single canton."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Aerial_image_of_Lake_Thun_%28view_from_the_east%29.jpg/330px-Aerial_image_of_Lake_Thun_%28view_from_the_east%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Thun","wikidata":"Q14426"},{"title":"Lake","blocks":[{"text":"A lake is often a naturally occurring, relatively large and fixed body of water on or near the Earth's surface. It is localized in a basin or interconnected basins surrounded by dry land. Lakes lie completely on land and are separate from the ocean, although they may be connected with the ocean by outflowing rivers. Lakes, like other bodies of water, are part of the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Most lakes are fresh water and account for almost all the world's surface freshwater, but some are salt lakes with salinities even higher than that of seawater. Lakes vary significantly in surface area and volume of water, but in total cover approximately 2.5 × 106 km2 (less than 2%) of the Earth's surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg/330px-Lake_Idro_Italy_2005-08-16.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake","wikidata":"Q23397"},{"title":"Thun","blocks":[{"text":"Thun is a town and a municipality in the administrative district of Thun in the canton of Bern in Switzerland. It is located where the Aare flows out of Lake Thun (Thunersee), 30 kilometres southeast of Bern."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/1_thun_castle_view_2012.jpg/330px-1_thun_castle_view_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thun","wikidata":"Q68978"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/1207_Thun.jpg/500px-1207_Thun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/1207_Thun.jpg/1280px-1207_Thun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/1207_Thun.jpg/500px-1207_Thun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1207_Thun.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/1208_Beatenberg.jpg/500px-1208_Beatenberg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/1208_Beatenberg.jpg/1280px-1208_Beatenberg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/1208_Beatenberg.jpg/500px-1208_Beatenberg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1208_Beatenberg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/1209_Brienz.jpg/500px-1209_Brienz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/1209_Brienz.jpg/1280px-1209_Brienz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars